[{:seon.task/name "the-binary-main-is-the-system-process"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. THE SIMPLE DESIGN (owner, 2026-11-16: only Flow for processes; fewer, clearer steps; what HAS to be there justified, the rest is baggage). PHYSICS (what forces the design): (1) fork() copies only the calling thread, so the fork point is single-threaded; (2) an LMDB env is not used across fork, so every process opens the store itself; (3) main has one writer (LMDB's write lock, Datahike's one writer); (4) forked memory is shared until written, so the fork point holds only what children read and children never write it (Jolt's collection ceiling, on main e0fd2346a7). FOUR PROCESS KINDS, EACH ONE FLOW GRAPH: the system process (the binary's main; one proc on its one thread inside jolt.host/main-carrier-run!, its create-flow given the fiber executor for every workload so no proc can start a thread; job: hold main's program and fork on request); the writer process (its first fork, today's root: writer, api, reconciler, effects; job: write main and serve); an agent (transport, repl; works one branch); a test child (an agent's fork; runs one test). THE PROTOCOL, THREE MESSAGES to the system process: fork (fork a child, answer its pid); commit (data's hold-main-schema!, then load-diff! of what moved; one full collection when the program moved); exit (reap). BOOT, TWO STEPS: the system process compacts the store if due (the one moment nothing else holds it), opens it, loads main's program, contracts, config and warm paths once, binds the writer process's listener, collects in full; then forks the writer process, which does every other start-up act (seed or catch-up, sending each commit as a commit message). WHY TWO LONG-LIVED PROCESSES, NOT ONE: if the writer forked agents, each would inherit the writer's caches, requests in flight and boot work, the private memory we remove, and the writer needs threads (commits, shell, model calls), which (1) forbids at a fork point. BAGGAGE DELETED: the writer process forking a fork server, fork-server-proc and its reader thread; the standby and promote (runtime deletes them, rebased on this task once the system process is on main, since today the standby is the fork server's only replacement short of a restart: tell runtime when it lands); the static freeze, preload! (both on main); seed and catch-up before the fork (they run in the writer process); the per-fork warm-start; per-agent start-up work, contracts re-armed (13.9 MB measured in a child), record and page rendered, config re-held (done once in the system process, inherited; an agent renders only when asked); load-all! beside load-diff! (one path from the binary's record or a db). STOP: the writer process ends with :stop: the system process reaps it, ends its group, exits; the writer process crashes or restarts: the system process forks a new one, agents keep running and reconnect through the one listener (flow's transport); :hand-over: the old system process exits, freeing the writer and the listener. BOUNDS: a fork 1-3 ms; the system process ~120 MB flat across merges; an agent's own memory <=5 MB plus whatever a raw fork forces. MEASURED 2026-11-16 (deps' agent k03ef8aa9dd1c247): a fresh agent 370.46 MB private, +0.05 MB after a collection, +0.02 MB after a read (result/dd2d0492); a raw jolt.host/fork-alone child is 28.2 MB private as its first act (result/ddd473b5), Jolt's after-fork reset or the OS, to measure first; a bare child after fork, collection, store reopen, connect and a read is 60.3 MB (result/b805798a). STEPPED BOOT FROM AN EMPTY STORE (deps, 2026-11-16, on main 58f6647342 + a debug build: each step SIGSTOPs its process, vmmap PRV/COW read on the stopped pid; vmmap's per-mode sum checked equal to its own TOTAL dirty, 545.9 MB both): root at its first line of root mode 544.5 MB private (420 MB heap in use, 21 collections: the binary before any boot code); store open +1.8; seed +1,056 (883 collections); hold-config, load-all, digest, contracts, warm ~0 net; collection before the fork 1,160; after the fork root 139 private + 958 COW, 3 threads -> 23, then 82 once Flow starts; the fork server 9.1 at entry, 227 after its own load-all! of the tests (+213, and its COW falls 1,026 -> 560: that load rewrites 466 MB it inherited); a standby forked at boot runs that load-all! again; a raw fork child 9.1-9.4 (not 28); root's embed-worker child 1.36 GB resident. Correction (runtime counted forms, 2026-11-16): the fork server's load adds only the test forms the binary lacks (6,651 live forms, 6,645 held by the binary record), so its +213 MB and 466 MB rewritten is loading the test namespaces at the fork point, repeated by the standby; not a second program load. The in-process e13_vm_dirty matches vmmap in root but not in a forked child (241 against 9.2): only vmmap from outside is the gauge until it does. Raw data: /tmp/seon-zero/steps. A FORKED AGENT'S OWN PAGES (deps, 2026-11-16, agent ga265cad25a9dab8 pid 75625 on the clean store, vmmap PRV from outside): 90.2 MB at spawn, 108.7 after (eval 1). Chez inside it counts 1,000 MB in use and 290 MB static, almost all inherited and shared. Lowering heap-reserve-ratio to 0.1 or release-minimum-generation to 0 returns nothing (108.9 unchanged; with release-min 0 the same eval still grows it). An eval making ~440 MB of garbage (111 collections) adds ~72 MB private a run (118.8 -> 201.6 -> 270.2 -> 342.7) while COW falls ~30 MB a run: the child's own collections and writes dirty inherited pages. An explicit full collection in the child (past Jolt's ceiling) freed 278 MB by Chez's count and RAISED private to 813 MB: it copied the parent's heap. The fork server collects only when the program moves (fork_server.clj:141,170,201), never before a fork, so what it makes between moves sits in young generations that agents do collect. Next: name what each run's inherited writes are (card marks, mutated inherited objects, young inherited objects), then make the fork point hand children nothing a child collects. PROCESS KINDS, SIMPLIFIED (owner, 2026-11-16: 'A test child is just an agent'; 'main' is a branch): two kinds. The system process (the fork point) and agents. The writer process is main's agent: the agent on the main branch, whose Flow graph holds main's writer (one writer per branch, as every agent's), the api and the reconciler. A test child is an agent forked from its branch's agent, which runs one test and exits. THE GAUGE (corrected 2026-11-16, runtime's finding, checked by deps): phys_footprint is NOT a process's own memory; it also counts dirty pages still shared copy-on-write with the parent. Fresh agent pid 63596 from outside: 225 MB = 52.1 MB private dirty (vmmap SM=PRV) + 172.9 MB shared (SM=COW). The 5 MB row is held to private dirty bytes: jolt.host/process-private-bytes (deps', Jolt): this process's regions summed by share mode, read inside the process, since another process's task port is not given on macOS. Measured with it to come: a raw fork's child (36 MB phys_footprint from outside, result/a0ae10e3) and a fresh agent's ~52 MB. Chez's release-minimum-generation lowered in a child does not change what 200 MB of garbage leaves (72 vs 75 MB, result/a6cd7061). PROOFS: seon.main-test/the-writer-process-is-the-system-processs-first-fork (every process's parent is the binary's main), and a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read and across a merge, beside 5 MB."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
